Date: Fri, 10 Sep 1993 16:14:05 -0800
From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
Subject: META: Any "Eudora" users having problems with List software?

>* Eudora is an offline mail reader for the Mac (and soon, Windows).
>Incoming mail messages can be sorted by sender, date sent, subject,
>etc. Very nice for high-volume mailing lists.
>
>Eudora sorted messages by original sender, not by the resending site
>(extropians@extropy.org).
>
>* After rejoining the Extropians list, on around 9-5-93, I discovered
>that all messages from the Extropians list were being sorted as having
>one sender: the extropians@extropy.org address.
>
>The mail headers still have the original sender listed, of course, but
>I cannot sort by sender, nor can I tell at a glance who has sent a
>message.

I bet you've upgraded your copy of Eudora in the interim.  I noticed this
same thing when I upgraded to version 1.4.

Here's the scoop as I understand it (I'm working from my memory of a
posting on this issue on comp.sys.mac.comm about a week ago).  There is a
resource 'STR#' 5400 in Eudora which specifies the "preference order" of
various lines which might appear in the header.  Default setting is
"Reply-To:" "Resent-Reply-To:" "From:" and "Resent-From:".  Eudora uses
this list both to determine what it displays in the In basket, but also to
determine who a response should go to.

The order of the entries, I believe, changed between 1.3 and 1.4.  A copy
of the resource is also stored in the Eudora Preferences file; this is why
1.3 now behaves strangely.

This is to say, Mr. Dorner has effectively screwed us by using one resource
for two different things.  You could reorder the list so that "From:" came
first; your In basket would then display correctly, but when you tried to
reply, it would go to the sender (i.e. the name on the "From:" line) rather
than the list (i.e. the name in the "Reply-To:" line).

>* By the way, the "Exi-Bay" list was giving me normal results up until
>the same time I rejoined the main list (a coincidence? or evidence
>that something at *my end* changed?).

No, a "Reply-To:" line is also used in the Exi-Bay list header.

>* My other mailing lists (notably Cypherpunks, others) are still
>sorting the way they always have--only the Extropians lists are
>behaving as I've just described.

There's no "Reply-To:" line in Cypherpunks headers.

>I forwarded some of the headers to Ray as I received them online, on
>Netcom, before Eudora saw them, and thene the same headers _after_
>Eudora has downloaded them. No obvious explanations occurred.
>
>So, is anyone else out there using Eudora? (BTW, the behavior
>described happens both with the new version 1.4fc1 and the old
>standby, version 1.3, which I've been using for 8 months.)
>
>Anyone seeing this behavior? Any suggestions?

Um, try trashing the Preferences file and fall back to 1.3?

Send death threats to Steve Dorner?

>I hope someone can help, as I cannot really live with a high-volume
>list like Extropians dumping all messages into one "sender."

It _is_ a nuisance...


