Date: Fri, 10 Sep 93 20:52 EDT
From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: PATENT: Gene Patents

The Sept. 4, 1993 issue of Science News has on pages 154 - 157 an
article on issues surrounding patenting of genes and gene fragments.
The news, in case you didn't already know, is that there is a patenting
frenzy among researchers decoding the human genome.  They are even
patenting small fragments of genes (ESTs = Expressed Sequence Tags)
for which there is no known function (yet).

This has some interesting implications:

  (1) I assume that I won't have to pay royalties to those researchers
      for using these genes in my own body, even though the article
      didn't mention that potential problem.  (I guess merely living is
      not considered a commercial application of those genes?)

  (2) Biotech products based on these genes likely will be both small
      and valuable, the patents will be public information, and gene
      sequencing and manufacturing machines will become both cheaper
      and faster.  Is there any reason to believe that those patents
      will be enforceable for their 17 year lifetime?  In particular,
      once gene synthesis machines get cheap enough, people will be
      able to create their own biotech products on their home synthesis
      units, provided they have the proper programming.  (And access to
      the proper programming will be readily available via BlackNet,
      if necessary.)  How long will this take?

  (3) Offshore biotech havens and manufacturing in locations that
      ignore US patent law should become very big business.  This may,
      however, prompt the US government to destroy these offshore
      manufacturing plants under some "War On Drugs" pretext, even
      though the real goal is just to protect the local monopolies
      granted by the patents.  Or am I exaggerating the risk?
      The US doesn't outright destroy bootleg manufacturers of PCs and
      chips although they do seek other kinds of sanctions.  But maybe
      that's because it's a lot harder to apply the "War On Drugs"
      banner to destruction of bootleg PCs and chips than to destruction
      of patented biotech products.

