I'm not sure what you mean. Buying a newspaper is more abstract than
explaining quantum physics? Money is built on language?

>in fact, something like the use of money could not even arise until language
>>and custom prepared a place for it.

Was this a necessity? I suppose so. Agoric systems may turn this on it
head, and achieve AI by assembling components that use money at a low level
of  abstraction. But you're historically correct, of course.

> Language, like tool-making, can be seen to be rooted in
>behavior we clearly class as instinctual, but the same cannot be said
>for money.

