orders have been formed, they can be "designed" to some extent, although this
intervention is more like rechanneling ol' Miss and damming it up than
building something from scratch.  This doesn't change the fact that they
aren't and can't be results of human design, only other kinds of human action.

>What justifies dropping money in with language and other
>culturally-evolved pragmas?

See the above mentioned books by Rothbard and Menger.

>They are all of prehistoric, indeed
>probably preliterate, provenance, but if you know of a definitive
>history or well-supported theory of the development of money that
>shows that Ook didn't have to *explain* to Og that Ook's four cowrie
>shells could be exchanged for one of Og's spearheads because Mogh had
>gone fishing that morning and would almost certainly trade a fish for
>two cowries[2] when he got back

Like I said, see Rothbard, Menger, Mises... the very concept of spontaneous
order was discovered by the Austrian economists during their research on the
origin of money.

Money is implicit in barter, and barter is pre-linguistic.  When the Hudson
Bay Company wanted to open up trade relations with Indian tribes on the shore
they had just anchored off of, they would lay goods on a blanket.  The Indians
would lay some goods of their own on a blanket.  The Hudson reps would either
accept the trade, or alter what lay on their blanket.  Then the Indians would
either accept the deal or alter what lay on their blanket, etc.  All this
without speaking a word of each other's language.

This may not be a proof, because each people did have their own language
already, but it is an illustration.

