You abuse the term "invention," which entails intentional causation of
necessity.  Things can be formed without concepts, but not literally made.
This is a case of confusing the metaphorical and literal uses of words.

We can form things without concepts, unconsciously, as occurs naturally all
the time, but these are not inventions, not "made" by anyone.  True, we can't
conceptualize anything until we've formed concepts, but they aren't invented,
either.  Their representations may be, but they are not.

>You're the one suffering from a fallacy here -- the fallacy
>that what you are doing is formal reasoning, when in fact you can't do
>formal reasoning on anything other than a formal system.

I've thought about this charge of your since you last made it against me,
and I've come to the conclusion that you're mistaken in placing formal
reasoning as primary to informal.  Formal reasoning is reasoning stripped
of referents, of intentional relation to concrete objects.  It is a derivative
form of reasoning, not a primary.  The primary form of reasoning is intentional,
referential, in relation to concrete objects.

What your accusation ultimately comes down to is the metaphysical denial of
the laws of Identity, etc., which are apodictic.

>This leads to conclusions like the idea that
>the evidence of your senses must be correct (we have proof otherwise

To be blunt, bullshit.  We do not.  Every such alleged proof I have ever
heard of is refuted in "The Evidence of the Senses," by David Kelley.

What we have are a lot of bad epistemological and metaphysical assumptions
which have hung on vestigially without being questioned and rejected and
have led to most of the failures of Modern philosophy and science.  What
we have is a lot of question-begging and hysteria.  We have no such proof.

>In concrete terms, what you
>claim is that there are a set of natural rights that can be
>established by pure reason.

I do no such thing.  The very phrase "pure reason" ought to be banished from
conversation until it can take its place as the historical curiosity of an
example of a philosophical failure that it is.  I'm no Kantian, no matter how
much you may try to interpret me as one.

This is nothing more than a strawman, whether you intend it as such or not.

I suggest you ask me what I think before you tell me what I think.  Since
you're merely being argumentative, I'm not going to take your flame-bait.

>What you have, Tim, is not a set of axioms and theorems. What you have
>is a model.

"Model" is another term that ought to be banished as above.  All it does is
confuse the issues.

>Now, I can model human rights pretty well by deriving them from
>decisions made in the context of human interactions. Rights tend to be
>legal schelling points selected by people in the course of daily
>interaction and later reified by custom.

This is nothing more than what I think, considered exclusively from an
external perspective.  What you seem to object to is that I like to integrate
this with consideration of the problem from an internal point of view.

You like to skip over the questions that arise from the internal, first-
person point of view, but I think this is a mistake.  Most recently, a form
of genetic determinism has emerged as the answer given by you and Dave
Krieger to the question of why we pick the goals we do, among other things.

How do you know?

This is yet another commission of the fallacy of self-exclusion, however.
If your preferences are the result of genetic determinism, then your claim
that your preferences are the result of genetic determinism is a result of
genetic determinism, and may or may not be true.  This is neither falsifiable
nor axiomatic, but a mystical article of faith.

You seem to think that I'm denying the role of custom in determining rights,
but that's not it at all.  I'm trying to address the fact that customs are
not irreducible primaries, but have their origin in our nature.

>Contract law as we know it is very much a human creation

It is not any kind of creation.  To use a term you may be more familiar
with, it is an emergent property.

>I find it interestingly inconsistant, by the way, that on the one hand
